  "notes": [
    "Scope: Worldwide merchant NAND flash (1989–2026) and stand-alone NOR flash (1988–2026), excluding embedded MCU flash macro area. 2026 values carry flag 'E' reflecting full-year estimates from H1–Q3 2026 industry tracking and full-year guidance.",
    "Unit identity & reconciliation: Since 1 EB = 10^18 bytes = 10^9 GB (decimal) and revenue is in US$10^9 (USD_B), blended average selling price satisfies price ($/GB) = revenue (USD_B) / capacity (EB). All years are reconciled to 2–3 significant digits with |price - revenue/capacity|/price < 3%.",
    "Early flash era (1988–1999): Intel commercialized NOR flash in 1988 (28F256) and Toshiba commercialized 4Mb NAND flash in 1989. Prior to 2000, NOR accounted for 85–97% of total flash revenue (used for BIOS, telecom, and 2G cellular XIP code storage). Early NAND revenues (1989–1999) are derived ('E') from WSTS/ICE total flash revenue (S7, S8) and Toshiba/SanDisk/Samsung NAND revenue shares.",
    "NAND vs. NOR crossover (2005–2007): Driven by MP3 players (iPod nano), digital cameras, and USB drives, NAND revenue ($10.8B in 2005) surpassed NOR ($8.1B in 2005) for the first time. Stand-alone NOR flash revenue peaked in 2006–2007 at $8.5B–$8.2B (S4, iSuppli) before declining as mobile phones shifted to NAND + DRAM MCPs, stabilizing at $2.1B–$3.5B in the Serial SPI NOR era (IoT, automotive ADAS, AMOLED, server BIOS/BMC).",
    "NOR capacity and pricing method ('E'/'I'): Stand-alone NOR is sold in low-density chips (1Mb–1Gb, where 1 Gb = 0.125 GB) for code execution rather than bulk storage, commanding a 5x–10x+ per-bit premium early on and ~800x–1000x per-GB premium over 3D NAND in the 2020s (~$85–$120/GB, equivalent to ~$0.011–$0.015/Mb). NOR capacity in EB is derived via revenue(USD_B) / price($/GB) and cross-checked against annual unit shipments (~4–8 billion units/yr at 32–64Mb average density in 2018–2026).",
    "Recent NAND cycles (2018–2026E): Reflects the 2018 3D NAND supercycle peak ($63.2B, 250 EB), 2019 oversupply downturn ($46.0B), 2021 pandemic peak ($68.6B, 570 EB), 2023 severe cyclical trough ($38.2B, ~$0.060/GB), 2024–2025 recovery ($66.5B and $78.5B; cumulative 2015–2024 shipments ~4.0 ZB per Yole), and the 2026E AI enterprise SSD supercycle ($115B, 1,050 EB / 1.05 ZB at ~$0.11/GB)."
